Let $C$ be a smooth projective curve over an algebraically closed field, and let $L$ be a line bundle (or, more generally, a fixed vector bundle) on $C$ used as a twisting datum. A twisted coherent system on $C$ (with twist $L$) is a triple $(E, V, \varphi)$, or more generally a pair $(E,V)$ where $V$ is a finite-dimensional subspace of $H^0(C, E \otimes L)$ rather than of $H^0(C,E)$; here $E$ is a vector bundle on $C$ of rank $r$ and degree $d$, and $\dim V = k$.

Recall that for an ordinary coherent system $(E,V)$ (the untwisted case $L=\mathcal O_C$) and a real parameter $\alpha>0$, one defines the $\alpha$-slope
$$\mu_\alpha(E,V) = \frac{d}{r} + \alpha \frac{k}{r},$$
and calls $(E,V)$ $\alpha$-(semi)stable if for every coherent subsystem $(F,W)$ with $0 \ne F \subsetneq E$ and $W \subseteq V \cap H^0(C,F)$ one has $\mu_\alpha(F,W) < \mu_\alpha(E,V)$ (resp. $\le$). This notion yields, for each $\alpha$ in a chamber of a finite wall-and-chamber decomposition of $\mathbb R_{>0}$, a projective moduli space of $\alpha$-stable coherent systems of fixed type $(r,d,k)$.

Problem. Formulate a definition of $\alpha$-stability for twisted coherent systems $(E,V)$ with $V \subseteq H^0(C, E\otimes L)$ that:

(i) reduces to the classical $\alpha$-stability when $L = \mathcal O_C$;
(ii) is preserved by an appropriate notion of subobject (specifying what a "twisted coherent subsystem" is, including the behaviour of $W \subseteq V$ under the twist by $L$);
(iii) admits a wall-and-chamber structure in $\alpha$ analogous to the untwisted case;
(iv) supports the GIT or moduli-theoretic construction of a projective (or at least quasi-projective) coarse moduli space of $\alpha$-semistable twisted coherent systems of fixed numerical type, with the $\alpha$-stable locus open inside it;
(v) behaves well geometrically, in particular giving the expected dimension and Brill–Noether-type loci, and is compatible with deformation and specialization of the twisting bundle $L$.

Provide such a definition and verify properties (i)–(v).

